---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
import Ornament from "../components/Ornament.astro";
import { withBase, BRANCHES, BRANCH_ORDER } from "../lib/site.js";
import { characters } from "../lib/portal-data.js";

const TRADITIONS = BRANCH_ORDER.filter((b) => b !== "reception");

const rows = characters()
  .filter((c) => (c.aka ?? []).length > 0)
  .map((c) => {
    const byTradition = {};
    for (const t of TRADITIONS) {
      const hit = (c.aka ?? []).find((a) => a.tradition === t);
      byTradition[t] = hit ?? null;
    }
    // The canonical name counts as the form in the tradition the portal files them under.
    const home = c.branch ?? c.traditions[0];
    if (!byTradition[home]) byTradition[home] = { form: c.name, tradition: home };
    return { c, byTradition, spread: Object.values(byTradition).filter(Boolean).length };
  })
  .sort((a, b) => b.spread - a.spread || a.c.name.localeCompare(b.c.name));
---

<Layout
  title="One name, many mouths"
  description="The same figures under the names each tradition gives them — Gwalchmai and Gawain, Peredur and Perceval and Parzival, Cai and Kay."
  current="/knights"
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <span class="rubric">Names</span>
    <h1>One name, many mouths</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A Welsh storyteller, a French poet, a German knight and an English prisoner are describing the
      same man, and calling him four different things. Following a figure across the tradition means
      following his name as it is carried into other languages and worn down.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read across a row and you are watching a transmission route. Peredur becomes Perceval in
      French and Parzival in German. Gwalchmai &mdash; hawk of May &mdash; becomes Gauvain, then
      Gawain. Caledfwlch becomes Caliburn in Geoffrey&rsquo;s Latin, and Excalibur by the time it
      reaches Malory.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data names-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Figure</th>
          {TRADITIONS.map((t) => (
            <th scope="col" data-branch={t}>{BRANCHES[t].label}</th>
          ))}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><a href={withBase(`/knights/${r.c.id}/`)}>{r.c.name}</a></th>
              {TRADITIONS.map((t) => (
                <td data-branch={t} class={r.byTradition[t] ? "has-form" : "no-form"}>
                  {r.byTradition[t] ? (
                    <>
                      <span class="form">{r.byTradition[t].form}</span>
                      {r.byTradition[t].note && <span class="form-note">{r.byTradition[t].note}</span>}
                    </>
                  ) : (
                    <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
                  )}
                </td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <Ornament />

  <section class="narrow narrow--flush">
    <span class="rubric">The object, too</span>
    <h2>Caledfwlch, Caliburn, Excalibur</h2>
    <p>
      The sword goes through the same process. Welsh tradition has <em>Caledfwlch</em>. Geoffrey
      latinises it to <em>Caliburnus</em> and says it was <em>forged within the Isle of Avalon</em>.
      The French turn that into <em>Escalibor</em>, and by Malory it is Excalibur &mdash; and by then
      it has also acquired a scabbard worth more than the blade, an arm in a lake, and an obligation
      to be given back.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is what transmission looks like from underneath: not a story being copied, but a word
      being pronounced by people who did not speak the language it came from, gathering attachments
      at every stop.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="narrow narrow--flush">
    <span class="rubric">A caution</span>
    <h2>Same name is not always same figure</h2>
    <p>
      The rows above assert that these names refer to one another, and for most of them that is
      uncontroversial. But a name surviving is not the same as a character surviving. Cai in
      <em>Culhwch</em> can hold his breath for nine days under water and generate enough heat to
      light a fire; Chrétien&rsquo;s Keu is a bad-tempered steward who exists to be unhorsed.
      Whether that is one character changing or two characters sharing a name is a real question,
      and the table cannot settle it.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Where the traditions differ about a figure, the difference is on their
      <a href={withBase("/knights/")}>dossier page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .names-table th[scope="col"] { color: var(--branch-ink); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--branch); }
  .names-table th[scope="row"] { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 0.98rem; white-space: nowrap; text-transform: none; letter-spacing: 0; color: var(--ink); }
  .names-table td { vertical-align: top; }
  .names-table td.has-form { background: var(--branch-wash); }
  .names-table td.no-form { color: var(--line-strong); text-align: center; }
  .form { display: block; }
  .form-note { display: block; font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--ink-faint); line-height: 1.3; margin-top: 0.2em; }
</style>
